  "worked_solution": "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x & =\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arccos}\u2061(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the $4$th quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/17803dc8d1d3ca2e31586969797b401d5d0a2d958bd6628387b512974076df7a.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $x=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
